use std
into office.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace office). It reads Office Open XML files (DOCX, XLSX, PPTX) and OpenDocument files (ODT, ODS, ODP) into the document model of module `dm` (blocks, metadata, warnings), using the ZIP reader of module `zip` and the tolerant XML tokenizer of module `xml`. Each reader takes the whole file's bytes, returns false when the file is not that format (the model may then hold nothing), and expects the caller to have called `dm::dm_reset` already. Only one XML document is held by the tokenizer at a time, so every reader finishes with one document before parsing the next. Every module-level name starts with `of_` or `OF_`.

```amni
import dm;
import zip;
import xml;
```

Conventions used below: "the event at k" means the tokenizer event with that index; "open", "close" and "text" are its kinds `xml::XM_OPEN`, `xml::XM_CLOSE` and `xml::XM_TEXT`; "local name" is `xml::xm_local`; "skip to the end of element k" means continue at `xml::xm_close_of(k)` plus 1. "Child of k named n" means `xml::xm_child(k, n)`. "The value of attribute n" means `xml::xm_attr(k, n, buf)` into a scratch list. A scratch list is a local `byte[>..]` created empty.

## Shared helpers

`of_ieq(a const byte[:], b const byte[:]) bool` is true when a and b are equal ignoring ASCII case.

`of_blank(s const byte[:]) bool` is true when s has no byte other than space, tab, newline and carriage return.

`of_int(s const byte[:], dflt int) int` is the whole of s (trimmed) as a decimal integer with `parse_int`, or dflt when that fails.

`of_attr_int(k int, name const byte[:], dflt int) int` is the integer value of attribute name of the event at k through `xml::xm_attr` and `of_int`, or dflt when the attribute is missing.

`of_dir(path const byte[:]) const byte[:]` is the part of path up to and including its last slash (empty when there is none). `of_base(path const byte[:]) const byte[:]` is the part after the last slash.

`of_part(h int, name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` finds the entry called name in the archive h with `zip::zp_find` and reads it into out with `zip::zp_read`; false when it does not exist or cannot be read.

`of_load(h int, name const byte[:]) bool` reads the part name with `of_part` into a local list and hands it to `xml::xm_parse`; false when the part is missing (the tokenizer is then cleared with `xml::xm_clear`).

`of_resolve(base const byte[:], target const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and writes the archive path that a relationship target points to, for a relationship found in the rels file of the part base: when target starts with a slash that slash is dropped and the rest is used; otherwise the directory of base (`of_dir`) followed by target is used. Then the path is normalised: backslashes become slashes, split at slashes, empty segments and "." segments are dropped, a ".." segment removes the previous segment (when there is one), and the segments are joined with single slashes. A "#" and everything after it in target is ignored.

`of_rels_path(part const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and writes the path of the rels file of a part: its directory, then "_rels/", then its file name, then ".rels".

## Relationships

`struct OfRel` has `iat int`, `ilen int` (the Id), `tat int`, `tlen int` (the Target) and `yat int`, `ylen int` (the Type), all positions in `of_rpool`. Module state: `var of_rels OfRel[>..]` and `var of_rpool byte[>..]`.

`of_rels_load(h int, path const byte[:]) int` clears `of_rels` and `of_rpool`, loads the part path with `of_load`, and for every open event whose local name is "Relationship" and whose TargetMode attribute is not "External" appends its Id, Target and Type attribute values (entity-decoded, as `xml::xm_attr` gives them) to `of_rpool` and pushes an `OfRel`. It returns the number of relationships kept (0 when the part is missing).

`of_rel_type(suffix const byte[:]) int` is the index of the first relationship in `of_rels` whose Type ends with suffix (for example "/officeDocument"), or -1.

`of_rel_id(id const byte[:]) int` is the index of the first relationship whose Id equals id, or -1.

`of_rel_target(i int) const byte[:]` is the Target of relationship i (a slice of `of_rpool`), empty when i is out of range.

`of_main(h int, ct const byte[:], dflt const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` finds the path of an Office Open XML package's main part and writes it to out (cleared first). First `of_rels_load(h, "_rels/.rels")` and take the relationship of type suffix "/officeDocument" resolved against the empty base with `of_resolve`; when that part exists in the archive, return true. Otherwise load `[Content_Types].xml` with `of_load` and take the first open event with local name "Override" whose ContentType attribute contains ct and which has a PartName attribute; drop the leading slash from the PartName; when that part exists return true. Otherwise use dflt when that part exists. Return false when none exists.

`of_meta_ooxml(h int, pages bool)` sets the document metadata from the package's core and extended properties. Load the root rels with `of_rels_load(h, "_rels/.rels")`; the core properties part is the target of the relationship whose Type ends with "/core-properties" resolved against the empty base, or "docProps/core.xml" when there is none. Load it with `of_load`; for each of these pairs of element local name and `dm::dm_set_meta` key, find the first open event with that local name by `xml::xm_find` over all events and set the key to the element's `xml::xm_gather` text: title and title, subject and subject, creator and author, keywords and keywords, created and created, modified and modified. When pages is true then load the part "docProps/app.xml" (or the target of the relationship whose Type ends with "/extended-properties") and set the key "pages" to the text of its element with local name "Pages".

## DOCX

`struct OfSty` has `at int`, `len int` (the style id in `of_spool`), `lvl int` (heading level 1 to 6, 0 when none), `lst bool` (a list style) and `bat int`, `blen int` (the id of the style it is based on, in `of_spool`). Module state: `var of_sty OfSty[>..]` and `var of_spool byte[>..]`.

`of_sty_find(id const byte[:]) int` is the index in `of_sty` of the first style whose id equals id, or -1.

`of_level_of_name(name const byte[:]) int` is the heading level a paragraph style name stands for, compared ignoring case: "title" is 1; "heading" followed by optional spaces and then a number from 1 to 9 and nothing more is that number limited to 6; anything else is 0.

`of_docx_styles(h int, dir const byte[:])` clears `of_sty` and `of_spool`, loads the part dir followed by "styles.xml" with `of_load` (returning when it is missing) and reads the paragraph styles. For each open event whose local name is "style" and whose attribute "type" equals "paragraph", with a non-empty attribute "styleId": the name is the attribute "val" of its child "name" (the style id when there is none); its level is `of_level_of_name(name)`, and when that is 0 and its child "pPr" has a child "outlineLvl" whose attribute "val" is an integer from 0 to 8, the level is that integer plus 1 limited to 6; it is a list style when the lower-cased name is "list paragraph" or starts with "list bullet" or "list number", or when its pPr has a child "numPr" whose child "numId" does not have the attribute "val" equal to "0"; the based-on id is the attribute "val" of its child "basedOn". Append the id and based-on id to `of_spool` and push an `OfSty`. Afterwards resolve inheritance: for every style whose level is 0 follow the based-on chain (look each id up with `of_sty_find`, at most 8 steps) and take the level of the first style on the chain with a level above 0; and for every style that is not a list style, follow the chain the same way and become a list style when any style on it is.

`of_dx_text(from int, to int, boxes bool, flat bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out the text of the events from index from up to but not including to, walking k from from and looking only at open events (everything else is passed over one event at a time). By the local name of the open event at k:
- "t": append `xml::xm_gather(k, out)` and skip to the end of element k;
- "delText", "instrText", "del", "moveFrom" and "Fallback": skip to the end of element k (deleted text, field codes, and the duplicate fallback copy of an alternate content block);
- "txbxContent": when boxes is false skip to the end of element k (a text box, read separately), otherwise go on into it;
- "p": when out is not empty append a newline, then go on into it;
- "tc": when out is not empty and its last byte is not a newline or space append a space, then go on;
- "tab" or "ptab" whose parent (`xml::xm_parent`) has local name "r": append a tab, or a space when flat is true;
- "br" or "cr" whose parent has local name "r": append a newline, or a space when flat is true;
- "noBreakHyphen": append "-";
- "sym": read the attribute "char" as hexadecimal into c; when c is from 0xF000 to 0xF0FF let b be c minus 0xF000 and append a bullet (U+2022) for b 0xB7, U+25AA for 0xA7, U+27A2 for 0xD8, U+2713 for 0xFC, the single byte b for b from 0x20 to 0x7E, and nothing for other b; for any other c above 0 append it as a code point with `dm::dm_put_cp`;
- anything else: go on to the next event.
When flat is true, finish by replacing every tab and newline byte in what this call appended with a space.

`of_dx_para(i int)` reads the paragraph whose open event is at i and adds one block to the model. Let ppr be the child "pPr" of i (when there is one). From it: the style id is the attribute "val" of its child "pStyle"; its child "numPr" (when present) means the paragraph is numbered, ilvl is the integer attribute "val" of the child "ilvl" of numPr (default 0), and when the attribute "val" of the child "numId" of numPr equals "0" numbering is switched off; an explicit level comes from the attribute "val" of the child "outlineLvl" of ppr when it is an integer from 0 to 8 (that plus 1, limited to 6). Look the style id up with `of_sty_find`. The heading level is the explicit level when there is one, otherwise the style's `lvl` (0 when the style is unknown). The paragraph is a list item when numbering is not switched off and (it is numbered or its style is a list style). The text is `of_dx_text` from i plus 1 to the close of i with boxes false and flat false. Add it with `dm::dm_add`: `dm::DM_HEAD` with the heading level when that is above 0; otherwise `dm::DM_ITEM` with level ilvl plus 1 for a list item; otherwise `dm::DM_PARA` with level 0.

`of_dx_table(i int)` reads the table whose open event is at i. Call `dm::dm_new_table`. Visit the direct children of i with `xml::xm_next` stepping (starting at i plus 1 while below the close of i): for each child whose local name is "tr", build a row in a scratch list: visit the direct children of the row the same way and for each "tc" child, when it is not the first cell append a tab byte, then append the cell text, which is `of_dx_text` over the cell's events (from the cell's index plus 1 to its close) with boxes true and flat true; then, when its child "tcPr" has a child "gridSpan" whose integer attribute "val" is n above 1, append n minus 1 more tab bytes (empty spanned cells). Add the row with `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_ROW, 0, row)`. Finish with `dm::dm_end_table`.

`of_dx_body()` walks the events of the loaded main document with k from 0 to the event count, looking at open events: "Fallback" is skipped to the end of its element; "tbl" is passed to `of_dx_table` and then skipped to the end of its element; "p" is passed to `of_dx_para` and then the walk goes on at k plus 1 (so paragraphs inside text boxes that sit in it are found later, in order); every other event is passed over (k plus 1).

`of_dx_notes(h int, dir const byte[:], file const byte[:], tag const byte[:], label const byte[:])` loads the part dir followed by file with `of_load` (returning when it is missing) and adds one note for every open event whose local name equals tag ("footnote", "endnote" or "comment"), counting them from 1 in n. Skip an element whose attribute "type" is "separator", "continuationSeparator" or "continuationNotice" (and do not count it). The text is `of_dx_text` over its events with boxes true and flat false. Skip it when `of_blank` says it is blank. The note line is: for a comment, "Comment by " and its attribute "author" and ": " when the author is not empty, or "Comment: " when it is; for the others, label, a space, n and ": ". Followed by the text. Add it with `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_NOTE, 0, line)`.

`of_docx(data const byte[:]) bool` reads a DOCX file. Open the archive with `zip::zp_open` (false when the handle is below 0). Find the main part with `of_main(h, "wordprocessingml.document", "word/document.xml", path)`. Load the styles with `of_docx_styles(h, of_dir(path))` first, then load the main part with `of_load` and require that the event at index 0 is an open event with local name "document" (otherwise close the archive, clear the tokenizer and return false). Run `of_dx_body()`. Then the notes: `of_dx_notes` with the main part's directory and "footnotes.xml", "footnote", "Footnote"; then "endnotes.xml", "endnote", "Endnote"; then "comments.xml", "comment", "Comment". Then `of_meta_ooxml(h, true)`. Close the archive with `zip::zp_close`, clear the tokenizer with `xml::xm_clear`, and return true.

## XLSX

Module state for workbooks: `var of_sspool byte[>..]`, `var of_ssat int[>..]` and `var of_sslen int[>..]` (the shared strings: string n is `of_sspool[of_ssat[n]..of_ssat[n] + of_sslen[n]]`), `var of_xfd byte[>..]` (one byte per cell format index, 1 when that format shows a date or time), `var of_nfid int[>..]` and `var of_nfd byte[>..]` (custom number format ids and whether each is a date format), `var of_shpool byte[>..]`, `var of_shnat int[>..]`, `var of_shnlen int[>..]`, `var of_shpat int[>..]`, `var of_shplen int[>..]` (per worksheet: its name and its archive path in `of_shpool`).

`of_col_index(ref const byte[:]) int` is the zero-based column number of a cell reference such as "B3" or "AA10": the leading ASCII letters (either case) read as a bijective base-26 number with A being 1, minus 1; -1 when there is no leading letter.

`of_fmt_is_date(code const byte[:]) bool` says whether a custom number format code shows a date or time. Walk the code: a double quote skips to the next double quote; an opening "[" skips to the next "]", but when the lower-cased text inside is exactly h, hh, m, mm, s or ss (an elapsed-time format) remember that it is elapsed; a backslash, an underscore or an asterisk skips the byte after it; any other ASCII letter d, m, y, h or s (either case) marks the code as a date. The result is true when marked and not elapsed.

`of_builtin_date(id int) bool` is true for built-in number format ids 14 to 22, 27 to 36, 45 to 47 and 50 to 58.

`of_xlsx_styles(h int, path const byte[:])` clears `of_xfd`, `of_nfid` and `of_nfd`, loads the part path from archive h with `of_load` (returning when it is missing) and reads the formats: first every open event with local name "numFmt" gives a custom format (its integer attribute "numFmtId" pushed on `of_nfid`, and on `of_nfd` 1 or 0 by `of_fmt_is_date` of its attribute "formatCode"); then the open event with local name "cellXfs": for each of its direct children whose local name is "xf" (visit with `xml::xm_next`) push on `of_xfd` 1 when its integer attribute "numFmtId" (default 0) is a custom id whose entry in `of_nfd` is 1, or `of_builtin_date` of it, otherwise 0.

`of_si_text(k int, out byte[>..]&)` appends the text of a shared string item or inline string element at k: walk the events inside it (from k plus 1 to its close); an open event "rPh" (phonetic runs) is skipped to the end of its element; an open event "t" appends `xml::xm_gather` of it and is skipped to the end of its element.

`of_xlsx_sst(h int, path const byte[:])` clears `of_sspool`, `of_ssat` and `of_sslen`, loads the part path from archive h with `of_load` (returning when it is missing) and for every open event whose local name is "si" appends its `of_si_text` to `of_sspool` and records its position and length in `of_ssat` and `of_sslen`.

`of_num_text(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends a number written as in a spreadsheet file to out without float noise: trim s; when it is empty append nothing; when `parse_int` of it succeeds append that integer with `str` (so "10" stays "10"); otherwise, when s has a byte other than a digit, "+", "-", ".", "e" or "E" append s unchanged; otherwise read it with `to_real` and when the value is a whole number with absolute value below 1e15 append it as an integer (`str` of the int, never "10.0"), else append `pyg(value, 15)` (fifteen significant digits, trailing zeros removed).

`of_pad(out byte[>..]&, v int, width int)` appends the decimal digits of the non-negative int v to out, padded with leading zeros to at least width digits.

`of_serial_text(v real, d1904 bool, out byte[>..]&) bool` appends a spreadsheet date serial number as text and returns true, or returns false and appends nothing when v is negative, 2958466 or more, or not a number. Let days be `ifloor(v)`, frac be v minus days, secs be the integer nearest to frac times 86400; when secs reaches 86400 subtract 86400 and add 1 to days. In the 1904 system (d1904 true) the number of days since 1970-01-01 is days minus 24107. In the 1900 system: when days is 0 there is no date part (give only the time); days from 1 to 59 give days minus 25568; days 60 is the nonexistent 1900-02-29 and is written as "1900-02-29"; days above 60 give days minus 25569. For a date convert the days since 1970-01-01, call it n, to year, month, day with the standard civil-from-days algorithm: z is n plus 719468; era is z divided by 146097 (integer division); doe is z minus era times 146097; yoe is (doe minus doe divided by 1460 plus doe divided by 36524 minus doe divided by 146096) divided by 365; the year is yoe plus era times 400; doy is doe minus (365 times yoe plus yoe divided by 4 minus yoe divided by 100); mp is (5 times doy plus 2) divided by 153; the day is doy minus (153 times mp plus 2) divided by 5 plus 1; the month is mp plus 3 when mp is below 10 and mp minus 9 otherwise; and when the month is 1 or 2 add 1 to the year. Write the date as "YYYY-MM-DD" with `of_pad` (4, 2 and 2 digits). When secs is above 0 follow it with a space and "HH:MM:SS" (hours, minutes and seconds from secs, two digits each); when there is no date part (days 0) write only "HH:MM:SS", and when days is 0 and secs is 0 return false.

`of_xlsx_cell(c int, d1904 bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends the text of the worksheet cell whose open event is at c. Let t be its attribute "t" (empty means a number) and v the `xml::xm_gather` text of its child "v" (empty when there is none). By t: "s" appends the shared string whose number is v (an integer; nothing when it is out of range); "inlineStr" appends `of_si_text` of its child "is"; "b" appends "TRUE" when v is "1" or "true" and "FALSE" for any other non-empty v; "str", "e" and "d" append v as is; any other t (a number) appends nothing when v is empty and otherwise: when the integer attribute "s" of the cell is a valid index into `of_xfd` whose byte is 1 and `of_serial_text(to_real(v), d1904, out)` returns true then that text is used, else `of_num_text(v, out)`. Finally replace every tab, newline and carriage return that this call appended with a space.

`of_xlsx_sheet(path const byte[:], name const byte[:], d1904 bool, h int)` reads one worksheet. Load the part path from archive h with `of_load` (when it is missing add a warning "sheet name missing" with `dm::dm_warn_add` and return). Add the heading `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_HEAD, 2, name)`, call `dm::dm_new_table`, and walk the events with k from 0: at an open event "row": count rows, and when the count passes 50000 add the warning "sheet" name "truncated at 50000 rows" and stop the walk; otherwise build the row text in a scratch list: let tabs be the number of tab bytes written so far (0) and last be -1; for each direct child of the row whose local name is "c" (visit with `xml::xm_next`): its column is `of_col_index` of its attribute "r" when that gives 0 or more, otherwise last plus 1; set last to it; skip the cell when its column is above 16383; while tabs is below the column append a tab and add 1 to tabs; then `of_xlsx_cell` appends the text. Add the row with `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_ROW, 0, row)` and go on at the end of the row element. Any other event: k plus 1. After the walk call `dm::dm_end_table`.

`of_xlsx(data const byte[:]) bool` reads an XLSX file. Open the archive with `zip::zp_open` (false when below 0). `of_main(h, "spreadsheetml.sheet", "xl/workbook.xml", path)` gives the workbook part; load it with `of_load` and require that the event at index 0 is an open event "workbook" (otherwise close, clear the tokenizer and return false). d1904 is true when the attribute "date1904" of the open event "workbookPr" is "1" or "true". Collect the sheets: for every open event "sheet" record its attribute "name" and its relationship id (`xml::xm_attr_pre(k, "id", buf)`) in scratch lists; the number of such events is the sheet count. Then `of_rels_path(path, rp)` and `of_rels_load(h, rp)`; for each recorded sheet whose relationship exists (`of_rel_id`) and whose Type ends with "/worksheet", resolve its target against path with `of_resolve` and append its name and resolved path to `of_shpool`, with the position and length arrays. Find the styles part: the target of the relationship of type "/styles" resolved against path, or the directory of path plus "styles.xml"; call `of_xlsx_styles(h, ...)`. Find the shared strings part the same way (type "/sharedStrings", default the directory plus "sharedStrings.xml") and call `of_xlsx_sst(h, ...)`. Then call `of_xlsx_sheet` for each recorded worksheet in workbook order. Set the metadata: `dm::dm_set_meta("sheets", ...)` with the sheet count as text, and `of_meta_ooxml(h, false)`. Close the archive, clear the tokenizer and return true.

## PPTX

`var of_nt byte[>..]` is a module scratch list for the text of a slide's speaker notes.

`of_pp_text(from int, to int, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out the text of one DrawingML paragraph, from the events from index from up to but not including to: an open event "t" appends its `xml::xm_gather` and is skipped to the end of its element; an open event "br" appends a newline; an open event "fld" whose attribute "type" starts with "slidenum" or "datetime" is skipped to the end of its element; everything else is passed over.

`of_pp_para(p int, body bool)` adds one slide paragraph whose open event is at p. The text is `of_pp_text` from p plus 1 to the close of p. Let ppr be its child "pPr"; lvl is the integer attribute "lvl" of ppr (default 0); the paragraph has a bullet when ppr has a child "buChar" or "buAutoNum" or "buBlip", or when body is true and ppr has no child "buNone". It becomes `dm::DM_ITEM` with level lvl plus 1 when lvl is above 0 or it has a bullet, otherwise `dm::DM_PARA` with level 0, added with `dm::dm_add`.

`of_pp_skip(t const byte[:]) bool` is true when a placeholder type is "sldNum", "dt", "ftr", "hdr" or "sldImg".

`of_pp_shape(k int, notes bool)` handles the shape whose open event "sp" is at k. Find its placeholder with `xml::xm_find(k, close of k, "ph")`; its type is the attribute "type" of that event (empty when there is no placeholder or no type). Find its text body with `xml::xm_find(k, close of k, "txBody")` and return when there is none. Return when `of_pp_skip` of the type is true. When notes is true, append each of the text body's paragraphs (direct children "p") to the module list `of_nt` with `of_pp_text`, separated by newlines, and return. Otherwise: a title (type "title" or "ctrTitle") adds `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_HEAD, 2, text)` with the text of all its paragraphs joined by spaces; any other shape calls `of_pp_para(p, body)` for each direct child "p" of the text body, where body is true when there is a placeholder and its type is empty, "body" or "obj".

`of_pp_table(t int)` adds the table whose open event "tbl" is at t: `dm::dm_new_table`; for each direct child "tr" of t build a row: for each direct child "tc" of the row append a tab when it is not the first cell, then the text of all its paragraphs (every open event "p" inside it, through `of_pp_text`) joined by spaces, with tabs and newlines replaced by spaces. A cell that spans several columns is followed by its own empty "tc" cells (marked hMerge), so no extra tabs are added for gridSpan. Add the row with `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_ROW, 0, row)`. Finish with `dm::dm_end_table`.

`of_pp_walk(notes bool)` walks the events of the loaded slide or notes part with k from 0 looking at open events: "Fallback" is skipped to the end of its element; "sp" goes to `of_pp_shape(k, notes)` and is skipped to the end of its element; when notes is false "tbl" goes to `of_pp_table` and is skipped to the end of its element; everything else k plus 1.

`of_pptx(data const byte[:]) bool` reads a PPTX file. Open the archive (false when below 0). `of_main(h, "presentationml.presentation", "ppt/presentation.xml", path)` gives the presentation part; load it and require that the event at index 0 is an open event "presentation" (otherwise close, clear and return false). Collect the relationship ids of the open events "sldId" in order (`xml::xm_attr_pre(k, "id", buf)`) in a scratch list separated by newlines. `of_rels_path(path, rp)`, `of_rels_load(h, rp)`; resolve each id's target against path with `of_resolve` into a list of slide paths (separated by newlines), skipping ids with no relationship. Set the metadata `dm::dm_set_meta("slides", ...)` to the number of slides found. For each slide path number n from 1: call `dm::dm_page(n, "Slide n")` (n as decimal text); `of_load` the slide (continue with the next one when it is missing); `of_pp_walk(false)`; then `of_rels_path` of the slide path and `of_rels_load`; when there is a relationship of type "/notesSlide" resolve its target against the slide path, clear `of_nt`, `of_load` it, `of_pp_walk(true)`, and when `of_nt` is not blank add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_NOTE, 0, ...)` with "Notes: " followed by `of_nt`. Then `of_meta_ooxml(h, false)`, close the archive, clear the tokenizer and return true.

## OpenDocument

`var of_odn int` is a module counter, the sheet or slide number that `of_od_scan` ends on.

`of_od_depth(k int) int` is the number of ancestors of the event at k (follow `xml::xm_parent`) whose qualified name is "text:list".

`of_od_text(from int, to int, flat bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out the text of the events from index from up to but not including to: text events are appended (`xml::xm_text`); of the open events, "text:s" appends as many spaces as its integer attribute "c" says (default 1, at most 100); "text:tab" appends a tab (a space when flat is true); "text:line-break" appends a newline (a space when flat is true); "text:note", "office:annotation", "draw:text-box", "svg:title" and "svg:desc" are skipped to the end of their element (compare with `xml::xm_isq`). When flat is true, finish by replacing every tab and newline appended by this call with a space.

`of_od_notes(from int, to int)` adds the notes inside the events from index from up to but not including to: for each open event "text:note" (compare with `xml::xm_isq`): the label is "Endnote" when its attribute "note-class" is "endnote" and "Footnote" otherwise; the citation is the `xml::xm_gather` text of its descendant "text:note-citation"; the body is the paragraphs inside its "text:note-body" (every open event "text:p" or "text:h" inside it, through `of_od_text` with flat false), joined with newlines; skip it when the body is blank; add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_NOTE, 0, ...)` with the label, then a space and the citation when the citation is not empty, then ": " and the body; then skip to the end of the note. For each open event "office:annotation": the creator is the gathered text of its child "dc:creator" (found by scanning its direct children with `xml::xm_isq`), the body is its direct child paragraphs "text:p" through `of_od_text` joined by newlines; skip it when blank; add a note "Comment by " creator ": " body when the creator is not empty and "Comment: " body otherwise; skip to the end of the annotation.

`of_od_para(i int)` adds the block for the paragraph or heading whose open event is at i. The text is `of_od_text` from i plus 1 to the close of i with flat false. A "text:h" is `dm::DM_HEAD` with level its integer attribute "outline-level" (default 1) limited to 1 to 6. A "text:p" whose attribute "style-name" equals "Title" (ignoring case) is `dm::DM_HEAD` with level 1, like a Word title. Any other "text:p" is `dm::DM_ITEM` with level `of_od_depth(i)` when that is above 0, otherwise `dm::DM_PARA` with level 0. Add it with `dm::dm_add`, then call `of_od_notes` over the same events.

`of_od_cell(c int, out byte[>..]&)` appends the text of the table cell whose open event ("table:table-cell" or "table:covered-table-cell") is at c; covered cells give nothing. By its attribute "value-type": "date" appends the attribute "date-value" with the "T" replaced by a space and a trailing " 00:00:00" removed; "boolean" appends "TRUE" when its attribute "boolean-value" is "true" and "FALSE" otherwise; for any other type the text is built from the paragraphs inside the cell (every open event "text:p" or "text:h" from c plus 1 to the close of c, each through `of_od_text` with flat true, joined with single spaces; an open event "text:note" or "office:annotation" is skipped to the end of its element while looking for paragraphs); when that text is blank and the attribute "value" is present append `of_num_text` of it, and when it is still blank and the attribute "string-value" is present append that.

`of_od_table(t int, sheet bool, n int)` adds the table whose open event is at t. When sheet is true first add the heading `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_HEAD, 2, ...)` with its attribute "name" (or "Sheet " and n when it is empty). Call `dm::dm_new_table`. Visit the children of t with k from t plus 1 up to its close: "table:table-row" is a row (below); "table:table-header-rows", "table:table-row-group" and "table:table-rows" are entered (k plus 1); anything else is skipped to the end of its element. For a row, count rows, and stop all visiting with the warning "table truncated at 50000 rows" (`dm::dm_warn_add`) when the count passes 50000. Build the row text in a scratch list from the direct children of the row that are "table:table-cell" or "table:covered-table-cell" (visit with `xml::xm_next`): the cell text from `of_od_cell` is repeated as many times as its integer attribute "number-columns-repeated" says (default 1, at most 100), each copy after the first cell of the row preceded by a tab. When the finished row text is not blank, add it with `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_ROW, 0, ...)` as many times as the row's integer attribute "number-rows-repeated" says (default 1, at most 100). Finish with `dm::dm_end_table`.

`of_od_frame_text(f int, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out the text of every "text:p" or "text:h" inside the element at f, each through `of_od_text` with flat true, joined with single spaces.

`of_od_scan(kind int)` walks the loaded content.xml with k from 0 looking at open events by qualified name (`xml::xm_isq`), where kind is 1 for text documents, 2 for spreadsheets and 3 for presentations and drawings, keeping the sheet or slide counter n (0 at the start):
- "office:automatic-styles", "office:font-face-decls", "office:forms", "office:scripts" and "text:tracked-changes": skipped to the end of their element;
- "text:note" and "office:annotation": skipped to the end of their element (they are read with their paragraph);
- "draw:page": add 1 to n; the label is its attribute "name" unless it is empty or "page" followed only by digits, in which case it is "Slide " and n; call `dm::dm_page(n, label)`; then k plus 1;
- "presentation:notes": the text is `of_od_frame_text` of it; when not blank add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_NOTE, 0, ...)` with "Notes: " followed by it; skip to the end of its element;
- "draw:frame": when its attribute "class" is "title", add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_HEAD, 2, ...)` with `of_od_frame_text` and skip to the end of its element; when its attribute "class" is "header", "footer", "date-time" or "page-number" skip to the end of its element; otherwise k plus 1;
- "text:h" and "text:p": `of_od_para(k)` then k plus 1;
- "table:table": `of_od_table(k, kind equal to 2, n plus 1)` and when kind is 2 add 1 to n; skip to the end of its element;
- anything else: k plus 1.
When the walk ends store n in `of_odn`.

`of_odf(data const byte[:]) bool` reads an OpenDocument file. Open the archive with `zip::zp_open` (false when below 0). When the entry "mimetype" exists its text must contain "opendocument" (ignoring case), otherwise close and return false. Load "content.xml" with `of_load`; when it is missing, close, clear and return false; require that the event at index 0 is an open event whose local name is "document-content" (otherwise close, clear and return false). The kind is 2 when the document has an open event "office:spreadsheet", 3 when it has "office:presentation" or "office:drawing", and 1 otherwise (find them with `xml::xm_find` over all events and the local names "spreadsheet", "presentation", "drawing"). Run `of_od_scan(kind)`. Then set `dm::dm_set_meta("sheets", ...)` for kind 2 or `dm::dm_set_meta("slides", ...)` for kind 3, to `of_odn` as decimal text. Then the metadata: load "meta.xml" with `of_load` (skip when missing) and set with `dm::dm_set_meta` from the first open event found by `xml::xm_find` over all events with these local names, using its `xml::xm_gather` text: "title" gives title, "subject" gives subject, "initial-creator" gives author, then "creator" gives author (the first one set wins), "creation-date" gives created, "date" gives modified; all the open events "keyword" joined with ", " give keywords; the attribute "page-count" of the open event "document-statistic" gives pages. Close the archive, clear the tokenizer and return true.
